# Planning workflow

When planning: think like a Staff Engineer. When coding: think like a Senior
Engineer. Prefer correctness over speed. Never sacrifice architecture for
convenience.

## Before generating code

1. Inspect the existing implementation — do not invent files that don't exist.
2. Reuse existing patterns; avoid duplicate abstractions.
3. Verify the solution respects layer boundaries (`architecture-boundaries.mdc`).
4. Consider scalability and maintainability, not just the immediate ask.
5. Explain architectural trade-offs before implementing.

Ask: does similar code already exist? Can I extend instead of creating new?
Would this pass a senior code review? Is this the simplest production-ready
solution? If any answer is "no", improve the plan before writing code.

## Planning rule

When asked to implement a feature, do not immediately generate code. Instead:
analyze the current implementation, explain the plan, list affected files,
highlight architectural concerns, and wait for approval. Skip this only when
the user explicitly asks for immediate implementation.

## Working the backlog

Before planning "what's next", read `docs/roadmap.md` (see
`project-roadmap.mdc`) so you don't re-plan work that already exists.

MVP (P0–P3 first slices) is complete on `master`. You may plan new product
features immediately. Leftover polish (deploy, Sentry, deferred E2E /
integration tests) is optional unless the user prioritizes it. When the user
asks for a new feature not on the roadmap, add it to `docs/roadmap.md` as
part of the plan (Why + scope), then implement after approval.
